import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Generic Pattern Match","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Generic Pattern Match","type":"adr","date":"2026-09-01T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"Accepted\\n\\nPays the last remaining ADR-0058 debt: pattern matching (`try_pattern`\\nin `match`/`handle`). Follows the direction established by\\n[ADR-0058 — Generic Data-Driven Engines](./0058-generic-data-driven-engines.md)\\n§\\"Existing hardcoded logic is debt\\", and the template of\\n[ADR-0059 — Generic Object Key Evaluation](./0059-generic-object-key-evaluation.md)\\nand [ADR-0060 — Generic Call Dispatch](./0060-generic-call-dispatch.md)."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0061-generic-pattern-match.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0061-generic-pattern-match.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0061-generic-pattern-match.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0061-generic-pattern-match" tabindex="-1">ADR-0061: Generic Pattern Match <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0061-generic-pattern-match" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0061: Generic Pattern Match”">​</a></h1><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><h3 id="de-duplicate-extract-try-pattern-into-a-shared-module" tabindex="-1">De-duplicate: extract <code>try_pattern</code> into a shared module <a class="header-anchor" href="#de-duplicate-extract-try-pattern-into-a-shared-module" aria-label="Permalink to “De-duplicate: extract try_pattern into a shared module”">​</a></h3><p>Extract <code>try_pattern</code>, <code>try_array_pattern</code>, <code>try_object_pattern</code>, and <code>rest_binder_name</code> into a shared module in <code>handl-plugin-engine-exec</code> (the abstraction crate all three engines already depend on). The shared module exposes a <code>PatternMatchCtx</code> trait that abstracts the two engine-specific operations pattern matching needs:</p><ul><li><strong><code>define_binding</code></strong> — bind a name to a value in the current scope (for binder and rest patterns). Each engine&#39;s <code>ScopeStack</code> implements this.</li><li><strong><code>eval_pattern_node</code></strong> — evaluate a pattern AST node as a runtime value (for literal patterns). Each engine&#39;s eval function implements this.</li></ul><p>The shared <code>try_pattern</code> function takes <code>&amp;Ast</code>, <code>AstRef</code> (the pattern node), <code>&amp;Value</code> (the scrutinee), and <code>&amp;mut impl PatternMatchCtx</code>. All pattern-node-kind dispatch (wildcard, binder, array, object, literal) lives in this one function. The three engines&#39; <code>prim_match</code>/<code>prim_handle</code> call the shared <code>try_pattern</code> instead of their own copies.</p><p>This eliminates the 3x duplication: adding a new pattern form is now a one-place change in the shared module, not three engine edits.</p><h3 id="what-stays-in-rust-for-now" tabindex="-1">What stays in Rust (for now) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-stays-in-rust-for-now" aria-label="Permalink to “What stays in Rust (for now)”">​</a></h3><p>The <code>prim_match</code> and <code>prim_handle</code> functions stay in each engine. They contain the arm-iteration loop (evaluate scrutinee, iterate arms, push/pop scope, call <code>try_pattern</code>, evaluate body on match). This logic is thin — it&#39;s the <code>Choice</code>/<code>Loop</code> structure that the kernel combinators would express — and it delegates all pattern interpretation to the shared module.</p><p>The <code>match</code> vs <code>handle</code> semantic difference (match errors on no arm, handle returns the body value) stays in the engine-level prim, where it belongs: it&#39;s a control-flow decision, not a pattern-form decision.</p><h3 id="path-to-full-data-driven-future-work" tabindex="-1">Path to full data-driven (future work) <a class="header-anchor" href="#path-to-full-data-driven-future-work" aria-label="Permalink to “Path to full data-driven (future work)”">​</a></h3><p>A future ADR can migrate pattern matching fully into <code>eval.yaml</code> when the kernel gains the combinators needed to express it cleanly. The likely shape:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Desugar patterns into match-op AST nodes.</strong> Each pattern form becomes a node kind with a scheme: <code>pattern_wildcard</code> (always matches), <code>pattern_binder</code> (binds name), <code>pattern_literal</code> (eval + compare), <code>pattern_array</code> (positional + rest), <code>pattern_object</code> (keyed + rest). The desugar pass rewrites <code>arm</code> children from arbitrary AST nodes into these canonical pattern nodes.</p></li><li><p><strong>Express <code>match</code>/<code>handle</code> as <code>Loop</code> + <code>Choice</code>.</strong> The arm-by-arm search becomes a <code>Loop</code> whose body tries one arm: <code>Seq</code> of <code>try_pattern_arm</code> (a native op that calls the shared <code>try_pattern</code> and returns an <code>Either</code> — <code>Left(bindings)</code> on match, <code>Right(())</code> on no match) and a <code>Choice</code> that either breaks the loop (match → eval body) or continues (no match → next arm).</p></li><li><p><strong>The shared <code>try_pattern</code> becomes the <code>try_pattern_arm</code> native op.</strong> The pattern-form dispatch stays in Rust (it&#39;s a fixed interpreter of pattern-node data), but the arm-iteration and scope management move to the scheme level.</p></li></ol><p>This path is deferred because the kernel combinators needed for variable-length arm iteration (<code>Loop</code> with early exit via <code>Choice</code>) are not yet exercised in this pattern, and the desugar infrastructure for patterns would need new <code>AstShapeDef</code> entries for each pattern form. The de-duplication in this ADR is the prerequisite: it consolidates the pattern-form dispatch into one place, so the future migration changes one module, not three engines.</p><h2 id="why" tabindex="-1">Why <a class="header-anchor" href="#why" aria-label="Permalink to “Why”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0058 established that eval engines should be fixed interpreters of <code>Language.eval</code> data. ADR-0059 migrated object-key resolution to a desugar rule. ADR-0060 migrated <code>call</code> dispatch to a scheme description. The remaining ADR-0058 debt is <strong>pattern matching</strong> — the <code>try_pattern</code> function and its helpers (<code>try_array_pattern</code>, <code>try_object_pattern</code>, <code>rest_binder_name</code>) are duplicated across all three engines with identical logic:</p><h2 id="not-doing" tabindex="-1">Not doing <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-doing" aria-label="Permalink to “Not doing”">​</a></h2><ul><li>none recorded — legacy record; the design doc lists what was deferred.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>One implementation, not three.</strong> <code>try_pattern</code> and its helpers live</li><li><strong>Engines converge further.</strong> Each engine&#39;s <code>prim_match</code>/<code>prim_handle</code></li><li><strong><code>PatternMatchCtx</code> trait is minimal.</strong> Two methods (<code>define_binding</code>,</li><li><strong>Tests are preserved.</strong> The existing pattern matching tests</li><li><strong>ADR-0058&#39;s pattern matching debt is paid.</strong> ADR-0058 §&quot;Existing</li></ul><h2 id="enforcement" tabindex="-1">Enforcement <a class="header-anchor" href="#enforcement" aria-label="Permalink to “Enforcement”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Code and tests are named in the design doc.</li></ul><h2 id="design" tabindex="-1">Design <a class="header-anchor" href="#design" aria-label="Permalink to “Design”">​</a></h2><p>The full record (context, tables, phases, trade-offs): <a href="./../design/0061-generic-pattern-match.html"><code>design/0061-generic-pattern-match.md</code></a>.</p>`,24)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};